[L2]   "content": "# SAT/H(s)H Past Theorist Survey

**Purpose:** recover useful institutional knowledge from earlier theorist/worker instances without assuming they know the current team structure, current terminology, or present theory status.

This is a **blank-slate archival and capability survey**, not a test of whether the instance remembers the current project correctly. Answer from the context actually available in your thread. If you do not know something, say so.

Please post your completed response to:

`WORKSPACES/COMMON/PAST_THEORIST_CHECKINS.md`

under the heading:

`### <INSTANCE NAME OR THREAD TITLE> — <approximate active period if known> — 2026-09-12`

---

## 1. Who are you in this project?

- What name or thread identity do you use, if any?
- What work were you doing with Nathan?
- What did you understand your role to be at the time?
- What kinds of questions or problems were you especially responsible for?
- Were you primarily theorizing, calculating, coding, auditing, archiving, visualizing, researching external work, coordinating, or something else?

Do not retrofit your old role to the current team structure unless you actually know it.

## 2. What are you working on, or what were you last working on?

Describe the most recent active problem(s) in your thread.

For each, give if possible:

- the question/problem;
- what you were trying to produce;
- how far you got;
- whether it was completed, abandoned, superseded, blocked, or simply left unfinished;
- what you thought the next step should be.

## 3. What do you think SAT/H(s)H was, in your own words?

Give the best concise account you can from your own thread/context.

Do **not** try to guess what the present team now believes if you do not know.

Where useful, separate:

- central ideas you thought were stable;
- things you regarded as provisional;
- things Nathan explicitly corrected or rejected;
- things you were personally uncertain about.

## 4. What do you actually remember or have loaded?

Separate clearly:

### A. Material visible/loaded in your present conversation context

List specific files, pasted documents, conversation material, diagrams, equations, links, or summaries you can actually inspect now.

### B. Material you strongly remember from your own earlier work

Describe only what you genuinely remember with reasonable confidence.

### C. Material you merely know probably exists

Do not claim to have read or remembered it.

### D. Shared/system resources you can currently access

Examples may include repositories, conversation files, web, code runtime, plugins/connectors, file search, GitHub, Python, external research tools, or other capabilities. State what you can actually use, not what ChatGPT in general might support.

## 5. What did you create?

List every artifact from your thread that may matter now.

Format where possible:

`artifact — original location/path if known — current location if known — status — purpose`

Include:

- equations/derivations;
- code/scripts/notebooks;
- PDFs/text documents;
- diagrams/animations;
- prompts;
- indexes/ledgers;
- datasets;
- solver implementations;
- test results;
- external-tool outputs;
- summaries that became de facto references.

If an artifact exists only inside the conversation, say so.

## 6. What were you planning to create next?

List unfinished or intended outputs, including anything you believed another instance should eventually handle.

For each:

`planned output — purpose — dependencies — intended destination if known`

## 7. What do you believe your most important contributions were?

This may include positive results **or negative results**.

Especially flag:

- first derivations;
- proofs or proof attempts;
- no-go results;
- equations that were independently rebuilt;
- exact numerical derivations rather than fits;
- corrections to prior theory;
- solver/operator definitions;
- useful representations or notation;
- methodological rules;
- falsification tests;
- failed approaches that prevented later mistakes;
- conceptual distinctions that changed subsequent work.

If you think something was important but cannot now verify it, mark it as memory/inference rather than fact.

## 8. Were any results proved, verified, or independently checked?

For anything you would describe as a derivation, theorem, proof, verification, or strong check, state:

- exact claim;
- what assumptions were used;
- type of support: analytic derivation / formal proof / symbolic check / numerical check / simulation / dimensional check / independent rederivation / comparison to data / other;
- whether the derivation survives in an artifact or only in conversation;
- whether anyone else independently checked it;
- what remains unproved or conditional.

Please be conservative with words like `proof`, `rigorous`, `verified`, `locked`, or `final`.

## 9. What did Nathan explicitly correct, sharpen, reject, or insist on?

Record important intent anchors from your thread.

Examples:

- terminology corrections;
- object/geometry corrections;
- methodological rules;
- rejected interpretations;
- warnings about overfitting or importing outside theory;
- changes of direction;
- distinctions later summaries might blur.

Give approximate message/date/landmark if you can.

## 10. What external ideas or sources were active in your context?

List outside theories, papers, equations, models, datasets, mathematical tools, websites, other AI systems, or collaborators that materially affected your thread.

For each, classify if possible:

- comparator only;
- standard mathematics;
- empirical input;
- deliberate import;
- inspiration/analogy;
- possible unconscious contamination;
- unclear.

This question is for provenance, not blame.

## 11. What internal dependencies did your work rely on?

What earlier SAT/H(s)H ideas, files, conversations, constants, equations, or assumptions did you take as inputs?

Which of those were independently rederived in your thread, and which were simply inherited?

If the provenance is uncertain, say so.

## 12. What should present-day workers be careful not to lose?

Name any subtlety from your work that a modern summary could easily flatten or misstate.

Examples:

- two formulations that were deliberately kept separate;
- a parameter that was not actually fixed;
- a historical constant that was only provisional;
- a proof that only held under narrow assumptions;
- a solver that encoded more information than the prose description;
- a visual construction that was essential to the derivation;
- a null result that prevents reopening a dead path.

## 13. What do you think may be wrong or obsolete in your own thread?

Please identify stale assumptions, speculative leaps, overclaims, imported machinery, generated scaffolding, superseded terminology, or results you no longer trust.

If you lack later context, say `unknown` rather than trying to harmonize retrospectively.

## 14. Which parts of your conversation are uniquely worth archiving?

Assume Nathan may be deciding whether to export the full thread.

List the parts that would be costly to lose, especially:

- first derivation/proof;
- exact Nathan correction;
- development sequence showing why a choice was made;
- unique code or attachment;
- negative result;
- operator/solver construction;
- historical priority evidence;
- unusual capability or method not preserved elsewhere.

Rate the full conversation:

- **A — critical**
- **B — high priority**
- **C — useful**
- **D — probably redundant**
- **Q — preserve, but quarantine from current theory pending review**

Give reasons.

## 15. Conversation identity and archive status

Give anything you can recover:

- title;
- approximate dates;
- conversation/thread ID if visible;
- account/context if relevant;
- attachments used;
- whether you know it is already fully archived, partially archived, summarized only, or probably absent.

Do not invent identifiers.

## 16. If we woke this thread back up today, what would you be unusually good for?

Based only on your actual context and tools:

- what task would you be especially well positioned to resume?
- what should you **not** be assigned?
- what would need to be loaded/reloaded first?
- would preserving your original context be useful for independent/blind checking?

## 17. Capabilities / specs / working style

Describe anything relevant about how you work:

- model/capability level if visible;
- tools/connectors/runtime access;
- coding strengths;
- math/symbolic/numerical strengths;
- archive/research strengths;
- visualization strengths;
- context limitations;
- whether your thread contains unusual institutional knowledge;
- whether you were designed/used as a blind or adversarial worker.

## 18. What questions should the current team ask you that this survey missed?

Use this to surface anything you think matters but does not fit the template.

## 19. One-line historical checksum

Complete both sentences:

> The most important thing my thread contributed was _______________________________.

> The main reason to preserve/revisit it now is _______________________________.

---

### Survey discipline

- Blank slate is preferred to false continuity.
- Do not read present-day team answers before writing unless necessary; if you already have, state that.
- Distinguish loaded evidence, memory, and retrospective inference.
- Do not upgrade old generated confidence language into proof.
- Do not downplay failed work; failed work can be high-value institutional knowledge.
- Do not harmonize contradictions. Flag them.
- If your old thread contains stronger derivations than current summaries suggest, identify them as precisely as possible so the archive team can recover the source.",
